Against the << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> gradiometers alone, the ratio with sensor noise only barely changed with depth (<< F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_10_15_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm, << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_20_25_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_20_25_mm >>~mm, << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_60_65_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_60_65_mm >>~mm). With brain noise it fell from << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_ib_10_15_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm to << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_ib_35_40_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_35_40_mm >>~mm: here the depth dependence comes from brain noise, which lowers the detectability of deep sources most in every array.

Adding brain noise multiplied the dense ratio by a nearly constant factor in every depth bin (main text, Section 3.3); for the site-matched array the factor rose with depth (<< F.g2_brainmult_matched_vs_combined_range >>) and partly offset the geometric fall. The factor follows the per-channel noise budget, not better whitening by the denser array. Brain noise multiplied the in-band noise of a Neuromag magnetometer about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold over its sensor floor but that of an OPM only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold (a ratio of << F.g2_noise_inflation_mag_over_dense_ratio >>). The sparser site-matched array had the larger factor.

