\subsection{The model against the measured Neuromag covariance}\label{sec:S3.4}

Each channel of the task recording had its mean removed and was band-passed, as one continuous record, to << F.rev_cov_band_hz >>~Hz with the zero-phase Butterworth filter of Section~\ref{sec:S3.1}. The measured covariance $C_{\mathrm{base}}$ is the empirical covariance, without regularization or projection, of the << F.rev_cov_n_windows >> windows from << F.rev_cov_window_s >>~s relative to the stimulus events (<< F.rev_cov_n_baseline_samples >> samples). The empty-room covariance $C_{\mathrm{er}}$ was computed in the same way from << F.rev_cov_n_empty_room_samples >> samples, and the measured brain noise is $C_{\mathrm{base}} - C_{\mathrm{er}}$. Both leave out the bad channel (<< F.rev_cov_bad_channels >>), as the calibration of Section~\ref{sec:S3.1} does, and keep << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_combined >> channels (<< F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_mag >> magnetometers, << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_grad >> gradiometers).

A sample covariance differs from the covariance it estimates, and we measured this finite-sample effect on surrogates of the recordings, << F.rev_cov_n_surrogates >> in the analysis band and << F.rev_cov_n_band_surrogates >> in each sub-band. Each surrogate multiplies the Fourier transform of the whole band-passed recording by one random phase per frequency, the same for all channels. This keeps every channel's spectrum and every cross-spectrum, and hence the covariance $C_{\mathrm{full}}$ of the whole recording, but not its non-stationarity. The covariance $C_i$ of surrogate $i$ was then estimated from the same baseline samples as $C_{\mathrm{base}}$, and that of an empty-room surrogate from the same samples as $C_{\mathrm{er}}$.

Recolored to the model, the surrogates show what an exact model would give when measured in the same way. The linear map $A$ for which $A C_{\mathrm{full}} A^{\mathsf{T}}$ equals the modeled covariance turns $C_i$ into $A C_i A^{\mathsf{T}}$, a sample covariance of the model with the recording's temporal structure. The empty-room surrogates were recolored to the modeled empty room in the same way, and in the sub-bands the background scale was refitted to each recolored surrogate as to the data. The variance, correlation, eigenvalue and detectability comparisons were repeated with these covariances, and their 2.5th to 97.5th percentiles over the surrogates are the exact-model ranges below. With << F.rev_cov_n_surrogates >> surrogates, these percentiles rest on the most extreme one or two values at each end, and with << F.rev_cov_n_band_surrogates >> per sub-band they are nearly the minimum and maximum. Each range is therefore itself uncertain, and only a value clearly outside it is read as departing from the model by more than the finite recording explains.

Only the background's scalar and the room field's << F.meth_env_terms >>~×~<< F.meth_env_terms >> coefficient covariance were fitted to this dataset. Everything compared below is predicted rather than fitted, but from the same recording, so the comparisons test the model within that recording, not in another subject or session. The model predicted the magnetometers' median brain-noise amplitude at << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured; an exact model would give << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_null_range >>. For the gradiometers, the scale fitted on half the sites predicted the other half's median amplitude to << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random halves (Fig.~\ref{fig:S3}B). Fitted on the inferior half, it predicted the superior half to << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >>, and the other way round to << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >>: the modeled brain noise is too flat from bottom to top.

