Neuromag's measured detectability of target $t$ is $d_{\mathrm{meas}}(t) = d(s_t, C_{\mathrm{base}})$ on the good channels, where $d(s, C) = (s^{\mathsf{T}} C^{+} s)^{1/2}$ is the detectability of the main text's Eq.~(1), $s_t$ is the target's noise-free field and $C^{+}$ is the pseudo-inverse of the rank-aware whitening (Section~\ref{sec:S3.1}). Because a covariance estimated from finite data inflates detectability, each target's $d_{\mathrm{meas}}(t)$ was divided by the correction factor
\begin{equation}
b(t) = \left\{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[\frac{d(s_t, C_i)}{d(s_t, C_{\mathrm{full}})}\right]^{2}\right\}^{1/2},
\label{eq:S-bias}
\end{equation}
where $N$~=~<< F.rev_cov_n_surrogates >> is the number of surrogates, $C_i$ is surrogate $i$'s baseline covariance and $C_{\mathrm{full}}$ the covariance of the whole band-passed recording, each restricted to the channels compared. The corrected measured detectability is $d_{\mathrm{meas}}(t)/b(t)$.

Over the targets, $b(t)$ had a median of << F.rev_cov_det_combined_bias_ratio_3dp >> for the << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_combined >> channels (5th to 95th percentile << F.rev_cov_det_combined_bias_p5_p95_range >>), << F.rev_cov_det_mag_bias_ratio_3dp >> for the magnetometers and << F.rev_cov_det_grad_bias_ratio_3dp >> for the gradiometers. The hybrid covariance of scenario E (Section~\ref{sec:S3.5}) is the measured empty room plus the modeled brain noise. It was corrected in the same way from the empty-room surrogates, with the modeled brain noise added to their $C_i$ and $C_{\mathrm{full}}$ (median factor << F.rev_cov_det_combined_bias_hybrid_ratio_3dp >>). An exact model, measured and corrected in the same way, gives a median measured-to-modeled ratio within << F.rev_cov_surr_null_corrected_combined_range_3dp >> (<< F.rev_cov_surr_null_combined_range_3dp >> uncorrected), the range that the finite recording alone produces.

Uncorrected, Neuromag's detectability with the measured covariance was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_measured_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_measured_ci >> times its modeled value; corrected, it was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >>, outside the exact-model range. The brackets are parcel-bootstrap intervals, which describe how the median depends on the parcels it pools. The magnetometers alone gave << F.rev_cov_det_mag_corrected_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_cov_det_mag_corrected_ci_3dp >> and the gradiometers << F.rev_cov_det_grad_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_grad_corrected_ci >>. With the heart's field removed, the ratio was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_noheart_corrected_ratio >>. With the hybrid covariance it was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_hybrid_corrected_ratio >>: the departure lies in the brain noise, not in the room.

By lobe, the corrected measured-over-modeled ratio was << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_cingulate_corrected_ratio >> for the cingulate, << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_frontal_corrected_ratio >> frontal, << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_insula_corrected_ratio >> insula, << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_occipital_corrected_ratio >> occipital, << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_parietal_corrected_ratio >> parietal and << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_temporal_corrected_ratio >> temporal. By depth, the median ratio was << F.rev_cov_det_depth_10_15_median >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm, lowest (<< F.rev_cov_det_depth_40_45_median >>) at << F.g2_depth_bin_40_45_mm >>~mm and highest (<< F.rev_cov_det_depth_60_65_median >>) at << F.g2_depth_bin_60_65_mm >>~mm. The measured noise thus favored Neuromag more than the modeled noise did, most for deep and cingulate sources. Adding noise, a positive-semidefinite term, to a covariance cannot raise detectability. A ratio above 1, as at << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_share >> of the targets, therefore means that the measured noise was lower than the modeled noise along the directions that matter for the target's field pattern. The measured excess over the model, by contrast, lay in a few far-field components, which the whitening down-weights at little cost to the signal.

