\subsection{Sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:S3.6}

Each analysis changed one element of the adult model (the joint runs, several) and recalibrated the background; intervals are from << F.meth_boot_noise_sens >> parcel-bootstrap resamples of one anatomy. The spike simulations were not repeated under these alternatives.

The primary model omits the cortex within << F.rev_ns_near_rule_mm >>~mm of the inner skull: << F.rev_ns_near_n_vertices >> white-surface vertices (<< F.rev_ns_near_share_valid_pct >> of the valid ones) and << F.rev_ns_near_area_cm2 >>~cm² of cortex (<< F.rev_ns_near_area_pct >>). Lead fields are not converged closer than about << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>~mm to the inner-skull mesh. The primary variants therefore add the cortex from << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>~mm outward as background noise, as targets (<< F.rev_ns_near_f2mm_n_targets >> of the << F.rev_ns_near_n_targets >> near-skull targets), or both. Removing near-skull sources from the background and from the targets have opposite effects, so the exclusion is not conservative by construction.

The dense ratio was << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_bg_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_bg_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> with the near-skull cortex as noise, << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> with it as targets and << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_both_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_both_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> with both, against << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> in the primary model. The site-matched ratio was << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_bg_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>, << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> and << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_both_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>, against << F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>. On the near-skull targets themselves, the dense ratio was << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_near_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_near_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. A refined single-compartment model, whose lead fields converge nearer the skull, repeated the check down to the inner skull with the same result (Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}B).

The colored OPM noise added 1/f components with corners at << F.rev_ns_col_corners_hz >>~Hz to every white level. The primary band-variance detector whitens spatially only; a frequency-resolved matched filter also whitens in frequency, with a flat (reference) or spike-wave signal spectrum. Under the latter, the dense ratio at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz was << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c0hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> with white noise and << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c1hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>, << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c3hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> and << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> with the three corners (Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}C, D), the last << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_vs_white_pct >> against white noise. The site-matched ratio fell from << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c0hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> to << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c10hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> at the << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner. With white noise, the frequency-resolved detector gives a slightly lower ratio than the band-variance detector because it also whitens Neuromag's colored brain noise in frequency.

The heart was modeled as a current dipole << F.wr_ns_ff_heart_depths_mm|replace(" and ", " or ") >>~mm below the head origin, or as a magnetic dipole at << F.wr_ns_ff_heart_mid_depth_mm >>~mm. The eyes were modeled as current dipoles at the two eye centers located on the sample MRI, computed either in the head model or in an unbounded conductor. Each source had three orthogonal moments. Their level matched the room field at the median magnetometer or the magnetometer brain-noise shortfall, and they were added to the cortical background or recalibrated with it.

After the << F.meth_env_terms >>-term projection, a cardiac source at << F.wr_ns_ff_heart_mid_depth_mm >>~mm left << F.rev_ns_ff_left_heart_cd250_dense_pct >> of its field energy at the dense array, << F.rev_ns_ff_left_heart_cd250_matched_pct >> at the site-matched array and << F.rev_ns_ff_left_heart_cd250_combined_pct >> at Neuromag (Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}E). At Neuromag this was << F.rev_ns_ff_left_heart_cd250_mag_pct >> at its magnetometers and << F.rev_ns_ff_left_heart_cd250_grad_pct >> at its gradiometers, whose response to a distant source is small to begin with. The eyes left << F.rev_ns_ff_left_eye_bem_dense_pct >>, << F.rev_ns_ff_left_eye_bem_matched_pct >> and << F.rev_ns_ff_left_eye_bem_combined_pct >> at the same three arrays. With sensor plus brain noise, unprojected, the ratios changed by << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >> (dense) and << F.rev_ns_ff_matched_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >> (site-matched) over all calibrations (Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}F). Every array sees the added far field in a similar proportion to its brain noise. After the projection, the ratios changed by << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_proj_change_pct_range >> and << F.rev_ns_ff_matched_vs_combined_proj_change_pct_range >>.

The joint runs placed the near-skull cortex in the background, leaving out its targets, which favor the OPM, and added a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner under the frequency-resolved detector. The dense ratio at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz was then << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>, and << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> with the heart and eyes added at the magnetometer-shortfall level (Fig.~\ref{fig:S5}A). The site-matched ratios were << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> and << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd15ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>. Table~\ref{tab:S4} gives the range over all << F.rev_ns_alt_n_rows >> configurations. At << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz its lowest dense value came from the band-variance detector with the << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner, so the joint run is not the lowest configuration at every level.

With the primary model, the dense ratio fell from << F.rev_ns_sweep_asd7ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >>~fT/√Hz through << F.rev_ns_sweep_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz to << F.rev_ns_sweep_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz, and the site-matched ratio from << F.rev_ns_sweep_asd7ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> to << F.rev_ns_sweep_asd30ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>. In the adult comparison's own sweep (<< F.meth_boot_g2_sens >> resamples; Table~\ref{tab:S16}), the site-matched array's interval lay above 1 at an OPM white noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_10 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.g2_sens_asd10ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.g2_sens_asd10ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>) and below 1 at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.g2_sens_asd20ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.g2_sens_asd20ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>). Break-even white levels, where the median ratio equals 1 under the modeled Neuromag noise, were found on << F.rev_ns_breakeven_grid_points >> log-spaced levels over << F.rev_ns_breakeven_grid_range >>~fT/√Hz for every comparator in both conditions named in advance (Table~\ref{tab:S4}).

